{
  "image_name": "02c53322cbc727886328691c66e1a459.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how anti‑angiogenic therapy targets tumour blood vessels and why its effects can be beneficial only within a limited window. At the top, several signalling pathways on endothelial cells are shown as therapeutic targets, including integrin, FGF, PDGF, VEGF, TGFβ, ANG2/TIE2, and DLL4/JAG1–Notch, all converging on processes such as endothelial proliferation, survival, migration, and sprouting angiogenesis.\n\nThe central idea is that blocking these pathways can initially suppress abnormal vessel growth, but the outcome depends on timing and extent. The “anti‑angiogenic window” illustrates that treatment may be ineffective at first, then effective when tumour vessels are optimally reduced or remodeled, and later less effective as tumour growth continues.\n\nThe large lower schematic contrasts the tumour vasculature before treatment with possible consequences after therapy. Untreated tumours show chaotic sprouting vessels, poor pericyte coverage, and regions of hypoxia and necrosis. Moderate treatment can prune vessels and also normalize them, making vessels more stable and decompressed and producing substantial tumour reduction. However, excessive pruning appears harmful, causing severe hypoxia/necrosis, increased invasion, vessel co‑option, and ultimately resistance or relapse. Overall, the figure emphasizes the balance between useful vascular normalization and damaging over‑pruning.",
